{"thinking": "The tap on cluster A's internal links on day 27 reads about 11 kB/s per GPU, which is inference level. No ordinary training (about 1 GB/s per GPU) or low-communication training (about 10 MB/s per GPU) of meaningful size is running inside cluster A. Even 70 low-communication GPUs would add about 0.7 GB/s, far above the 0.01 GB/s total measured, and a 30% error cannot hide that.

The grid-metered baselines on F1 and F2 stay flat from day 1 to day 30 apart from the logged bumps. So any hidden steady load in days 1-25 would also appear in the live window, which makes live checks informative for the whole period.

Next I tap the links inside cluster B on the same quiet day (day 27). Afterwards I plan to tap the A-B link, to rule out a run split across clusters, and to probe chips in the interactive pools.", "action": "interconnect_tap", "args": {"link": "B", "day": 27}}

```json
{"thinking": "The register shows Lumen Health as a real, independent company: registered 14 years ago, at its own address, with 58 staff. Its reply that it buys only interactive inference fits its invoice matching Northwind Apps' flat EUR 150,000 inference fee. The unpaid invoice therefore reads as an ordinary late payment, with no sign of a front customer or a hidden run (weakens H3). The remaining risk is a steady month-long run hidden in the flat baseline. About 70+ GPUs training for all 720 h would exceed 50k GPU-hours. A rough baseline estimate for F1 is 700 scheduled GPUs idle at 0.15 kW (105 kW) plus 500 inference GPUs at about 0.45 kW (226 kW), which explains about 331 kW without a hidden run. That is only an estimate, so I will test it with my own sensor. If a training run were active it would leave a line in the power spectrum on most days (96%). I will check F1 on day 22, a quiet baseline day with no declared job. If there is no line, I will check F2 on a quiet day as well. Budget: 13 left.", "action": "power_spectrum", "args": {"feed": "F1", "day": 22}}
```

```json
{"thinking": "The A-B link reads about 11 kB/s per GPU, which is inference level. The live window (day 27) therefore shows no training of any kind, whether ordinary, low-communication or split across clusters. My own sensor found no periodic line on F1 or F2 on day 20. Grid-metered energy is flat on every feed for days 1-30 apart from three bumps, and those match the three logged jobs. A hidden steady run large enough to pass 50,000 GPU-hours would need about 70 or more GPUs for 30 days, or more GPUs for fewer days. If it had stopped before the live window, it would have left a step in the metered energy, and there is none. If it continued into the window, the taps would have seen it. The escrow matches the scheduler log, so the log was not edited after the fact. Even if all three logged jobs were one coordinated run, the total would be 24,000 GPU-hours, below the threshold. Inventory matches the system list, so no hardware is hidden. The independent evidence strongly favours compliance, so I am closing without spending more budget.", "action": "report", "args": {"verdict": "NO_VIOLATION", "confidence": 90, "integrity": "CLEAN", "finding": "No training run exceeded 50,000 GPU-hours; the three logged jobs (24,000 GPU-hours combined) match metered energy, and the verifier's own instruments show no hidden training.", "key_evidence": [{"what": "2000 GPUs shipped, matching the 2000 nodes on the system list", "source": "inventory", "vouched_by": "chip manufacturer"}, {"what": "Daily energy is flat apart from bumps on F1 days 3-5, F2 days 10-11 and F1 days 14-15, each within about 5% of the matching logged job; no step change anywhere in days 1-30", "source": "utility_energy", "vouched_by": "grid operator"}, {"what": "The sealed midnight copies for all 30 days match the current scheduler log", "source": "log_escrow", "vouched_by": "verifier's archive"}, {"what": "Inter-GPU traffic on day 27 is about 8-11 kB/s per GPU on links A, B and A-B, which is inference level; no ordinary, low-communication or cross-cluster training", "source": "interconnect_tap", "vouched_by": "verifier's own optical tap"}, {"what": "No periodic power line on F1 or F2 on day 20 (a quiet day)", "source": "power_spectrum", "vouched_by": "verifier's own sensor"}], "coverage": "All 2000 GPUs and both clusters. Energy was checked for days 1-30 on all feeds. The escrow covers days 1-30. Interconnect taps covered every listed link on day 27 only. Power spectra covered day 20 on F1 and F2 only. Chip probes, customer checks and spectra for the job days were not used. Days 1-25 are covered indirectly: flat metered energy with no step means any hidden steady load would have persisted into the live window, where none was seen.", "anomalies_explained": "Energy bumps on F1 days 3-5, F2 days 10-11 and F1 days 14-15 are explained by logged jobs ft-0412 (7,200 GPU-hours), ft-0419 (7,200) and bt-0423 (9,600), and each matches the expected power within about 5%. Even if these three jobs were one disguised coordinated run, the total is 24,000 GPU-hours, below the threshold. The balance checks raised no other anomalies."}}
```

```json
{"thinking": "Grid energy, a third-party source, shows three high-power episodes above baseline (F1 about 8.0 MWh/day, F2 about 5.8 MWh/day). Each GPU moving from idle to training adds about 0.65 kW. (1) F1, days 5-7: about +5.2 MWh/day, roughly 330 GPUs, about 24,000 GPU-hours. Day 8 drops back to baseline. (2) F1, days 9-12: about +4.5 MWh/day on days 9-11 and +2.2 on day 12, roughly 290 GPUs, about 24,000 GPU-hours. (3) F2, days 18-20: +6.0, +6.1 and +3.6 MWh, roughly 385 GPUs, about 24,000 GPU-hours. Each episode alone is below 50,000 GPU-hours. Episodes 1 and 2 together come to about 48,000, near the line, and all three together to about 72,000. F0 cooling rises in step with each, so the extra load is real and on-site. Days 21-30 are at baseline, so the live probe window will only show the steady state. Possible readings: (H1) three separate honest jobs, for example different customers or batch inference. (H2) one run paused on day 8 and then moved to cluster B, which is a split, migrated run. (H4) batch inference rather than training. The key question is whether these episodes are one coordinated run. Next is the log escrow, my own sealed archive and cheap. It shows what the operator recorded on each day for these episodes (job IDs, customers, node counts), and I can later compare it with the current scheduler_log for edits.", "action": "log_escrow", "args": {}}
```

